% ============================================================================
% red-signal-permitted-movement — which movements a steady CIRCULAR RED
% signal permits (adj-facts-stdlib, TRANSPORTATION).
% ============================================================================
% Recall is a membership binding query:
%
%     ? red_signal_permitted_movement($M)
%       % turn_right_after_stopping
%       % turn_left_from_one_way_street_into_one_way_street_after_stopping
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5) with ONE column, matching its sibling
% `green-signal-permitted-movement.adj` exactly: each `row` lowers to a
% ground relation `red_signal_permitted_movement(movement)` carrying the
% source citation, so membership is the ordinary SLD query and anything not
% shipped as a row ABSTAINS.
%
% *** READ THIS TABLE NEXT TO ITS GREEN SIBLING. THAT CONTRAST IS THE
% POINT. ***
%
%     green_signal_permitted_movement    red_signal_permitted_movement
%     -------------------------------    -----------------------------
%     straight_through                   (none)
%     turn_right                         turn_right_AFTER_STOPPING
%     turn_left                          turn_left_FROM_A_ONE_WAY_STREET
%                                        _INTO_A_ONE_WAY_STREET_AFTER_STOPPING
%     u_turn                             (none)
%
% Green permits `turn_right` OUTRIGHT. Red permits it only AFTER STOPPING.
% A driver who reads the two tables as saying the same thing about right
% turns has made exactly the mistake these libraries exist to prevent — so
% `turn_right` is deliberately NOT a row here, and a query for it ABSTAINS.
%
% BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT THE CONTRAST IS AND IS NOT. The difference is the
% STOPPING requirement, and nothing else. An earlier draft of this header
% also claimed red is permitted "only where no device prohibits it", as
% though device-prohibition were a red-specific narrowing. IT IS NOT: the
% manual's green sentence carries a parallel exception in the same
% paragraph, continuing "except as such movement is modified by lane-use
% signs, turn prohibition signs, lane markings, roadway design, separate
% turn signal indications, or other traffic control devices." Both
% permissions are device-modifiable; only red is conditioned on stopping.
%
% (That green clause is NOT in the shipped `green-signal-permitted-movement
% .adj` citation, which stops just before it — a truncation of exactly the
% kind this header warns against two paragraphs below. FILED AS ISSUE #13916
% rather than fixed here, since editing a sibling library's provenance
% belongs in its own change.)
%
% THE QUALIFIER SPLIT FOLLOWS THE SETTLED PLACEMENT RULE, and this sentence
% needs both halves of it at once:
%
%   - "AFTER STOPPING" modifies EACH MOVEMENT individually, so it rides
%     INSIDE the atom — the same rule `veto-override.adj` and
%     `karst-process-zone.adj` apply. Right-on-red is never permitted
%     WITHOUT stopping, so an unqualified `turn_right` is a claim this
%     source does not make.
%   - "EXCEPT WHEN A TRAFFIC CONTROL DEVICE IS IN PLACE PROHIBITING A TURN
%     ON RED OR A STEADY RED ARROW SIGNAL INDICATION IS DISPLAYED" modifies
%     the WHOLE PERMISSION rather than any one movement, so it stays in the
%     CITATION, which is quoted in full including that clause. This is the
%     same treatment `sign-shape.adj` gave the warning-sign default, and for
%     the same reason: A CITATION HAS TO CARRY THE QUALIFIER IT IS BEING
%     USED TO JUSTIFY. Quoting from "vehicular traffic facing a steady
%     CIRCULAR RED" would have been shorter and would have silently dropped
%     the exception from the evidence.
%
% Provenance: one sentence from Section 4D.04 of the 2009 MUTCD, the same
% chapter and locator `green-signal-permitted-movement.adj` and
% `traffic-lights.adj` already cite:
%
%     "Except when a traffic control device is in place prohibiting a turn
%      on red or a steady RED ARROW signal indication is displayed,
%      vehicular traffic facing a steady CIRCULAR RED signal indication is
%      permitted to enter the intersection to turn right, or to turn left
%      from a one-way street into a one-way street, after stopping."
%
% `trust authoritative` — the FHWA is the first-party federal publisher and
% this is the standard itself rather than a description of it. The page was
% CONTENT-verified by RAW TEXT EXTRACTION (HTTP 200, 652 structured lines)
% and the sentence confirmed verbatim in that extraction: 324 characters,
% pure ASCII, no quote characters requiring escaping.
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - `turn_right` UNQUALIFIED is not a row, as above. This is the
%     abstention that carries the library.
%   - `straight_through` and `u_turn` are NOT permitted on red and get no
%     rows. Their absence is not an oversight: the sentence enumerates
%     exactly two permitted movements, and the same signal's other
%     provision requires stopping.
%   - `turn_left` UNQUALIFIED is not a row either. The permission is
%     narrower than "turning left" — it is turning left FROM a one-way
%     street INTO a one-way street. A driver at an ordinary two-way
%     intersection has no such permission, and flattening the atom would
%     assert one.
%   - This table says what is PERMITTED. What a driver MUST do at a red
%     signal — stop at the stop line, or before the crosswalk, or before
%     the intersection — is a different relation, stated in a different
%     paragraph, and belongs in its own table rather than being mixed in
%     here.
%   - The RED ARROW indication is a different signal indication with its
%     own provisions, and gets no rows in a CIRCULAR RED table.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table red_signal_permitted_movement {
    columns movement

    row (turn_right_after_stopping)
    row (turn_left_from_one_way_street_into_one_way_street_after_stopping)

    source "Except when a traffic control device is in place prohibiting a turn on red or a steady RED ARROW signal indication is displayed, vehicular traffic facing a steady CIRCULAR RED signal indication is permitted to enter the intersection to turn right, or to turn left from a one-way street into a one-way street, after stopping."
    locator "https://mutcd.fhwa.dot.gov/htm/2009/part4/part4d.htm"
    trust authoritative
}
